 \begin{abstract}
-Functional connectome classification requires balancing discrimination against feature reduction. We evaluate a sparse-to-dense framework combining anatomical incidence gating, pairwise logistic ranking, support-restricted ridge refitting, and dense ridge ranking. An analytical result characterizes the gate's preference for shared-endpoint supports. On 54 participants at two parcellation resolutions, repeated nested cross-validation gives dense ridge ranking the highest mean ROC-AUC: 0.7940 and 0.8229 at 83 and 129 regions, versus 0.7491 and 0.7929 for RFE--SVM. Gated logistic learning leads the tested sparsifying pipelines at 0.7612 and 0.7977. Support reduction and selection stability provide secondary trade-offs. These exploratory same-cohort results favor dense regularized ranking for prediction and motivate independent validation of the sparse alternatives.
