 \section{Introduction and objective}
 Resting-state functional MRI provides thousands of correlated connections per participant, often in cohorts much smaller than the feature dimension. Feature selection can aid interpretation, but removing connections can also discard distributed predictive information. The relevant question is therefore how discrimination changes across sparse and dense predictors, rather than whether a sparse model improves every outcome.
 
-Our primary objective is to rank schizophrenia-spectrum participants above healthy controls, measured by held-out ROC-AUC. Feature count and support reproducibility are secondary outcomes. We compare incidence-gated logistic learning (IGL), incidence-gated ranking (IGR), ranking with support-restricted ridge refit (IGR-R), and dense ridge ranking (DRR), alongside LASSO, elastic net, and RFE--SVM. DRR retains every input edge and is the dense endpoint of this comparison.
+Our primary objective is to rank schizophrenia-spectrum participants above healthy controls, measured by held-out ROC-AUC. Feature count and support reproducibility are secondary outcomes. We compare incidence-gated logistic learning (IGL), incidence-gated ranking (IGR), ranking with support-restricted ridge refit (IGR-R), and dense ridge ranking (DRR), alongside LASSO, elastic net, and RFE--SVM. DRR retains every input edge and is the dense endpoint of this comparison. We evaluate both whole-brain and cortical-only connectomes.
 
 The contribution is a \emph{connectome-specific incidence formulation, analytical characterization, ranking/refit integration, and sparse-to-dense evaluation}. The incidence norm, pairwise ranking loss, ridge regularization, and refitting are established ingredients; none is claimed individually new. The row-space ridge reduction is included as a computational identity. Figure~\ref{fig:framework} summarizes the evaluated branches.
 
