 \section{Results and discussion}
-\subsection{Dense ranking leads prediction}
+\subsection{Whole-brain prediction}
 DRR has the highest overall mean AUC (Table~\ref{tab:primary}, Fig.~\ref{fig:sparse-dense}): 0.7940 and 0.8229, versus RFE--SVM's 0.7491 and 0.7929, differences of 0.0449 and 0.0300. It also leads mean BA and F1. DRR exceeds IGL, the best sparsifying pipeline, by 0.0328 and 0.0252, using all connections. These descriptive gains do not establish clinical or statistical superiority.
 
-\subsection{Secondary sparse-model trade-offs}
+\subsection{Whole-brain sparse-model trade-offs}
 IGL has the highest sparse-pipeline AUC, 0.7612 and 0.7977, with 1,029.4 and 1,690.7 mean edges. IGR-R uses 643.9 and 839.8 edges: 37.5\% and 50.3\% fewer than IGL, with AUC decreases of 0.0003 and 0.0041. Relative to DRR, it removes 81.1\% and 89.8\% of input edges with AUC decreases of 0.0331 and 0.0293. It has the highest F1 among tested sparsifying pipelines at both scales, although it uses more features than RFE--SVM and has lower specificity.
 
 IGR has the highest adjusted selection stability, $\widehat\Phi=0.3478$ and $0.3618$. IGL instead leads raw Jaccard; its denser supports partly explain this distinction. LASSO gives the smallest primary supports. Thus discrimination, feature reduction, and reproducibility favor different choices. Separately tuned selector/refit models do not constitute a fixed-mask causal ablation.
 
+\subsection{With and without subcortical connections}
+On matching folds (Table~\ref{tab:cortical}), cortical DRR leads AUC, BA, and F1 at both scales; its AUC falls by 0.0721 and 0.0378. Restriction lowers mean AUC in 15 of 16 primary comparisons; LASSO at 129 regions improves by 0.0070.
+
+Among cortical sparse pipelines, IGR-R leads at 68 regions: AUC 0.6752 versus RFE--SVM's 0.6475, using 414.2 versus 370.4 edges. At 114 regions, RFE--SVM leads at 0.7588 versus IGR-R's 0.7554, with 437.3 versus 583.4 edges. Adjusted stability favors IGR-R at 68 regions (0.2678) and elastic net at 114 (0.2895 versus IGR's 0.2886). Sparse-model advantages depend on representation.
+
 \subsection{Interpretation and limitations}
-Proposition~\ref{prop:star} characterizes a penalty preference; support consistency and capacities do not establish biomarkers. DRR's advantage is compatible with useful distributed information, but cannot identify its biological cause or isolate ranking from ridge and tuning effects.
